# Individual Reflection – Holiday Maker

## Technical Choices and What I Learned

When I started this course, I had almost no experience with databases or MySQL. At first, it was difficult to understand how databases worked and how the different parts were connected. Comparing databases to Excel helped me understand the structure and made it easier to get started.

I started by learning SQL and practising how to create tables, add information and retrieve data. I also used the course book Studera SQL – prata med data to understand the different concepts.

Working with Homenet also gave me another example to learn from and helped me understand how databases can be organised in different ways.

One important decision was how to organise the database. I wanted the tables to have clear purposes and avoid storing the same information in several places. For example, I kept bookings in a separate table because one customer can have several bookings and the same room can be booked during different periods. This also makes it easier to connect customers, rooms and bookings using foreign keys.

I used a junction table called hotel_x_amenities to connect Hotels and Amenities. I chose this solution because one hotel can have several amenities, and the same amenity can belong to several hotels. Without a junction table, the information would be more difficult to organise without repeating data.

I also made some changes to the original model. I changed distance_to_city to distance_to_center because it better matches the project requirements. I changed id_price_per_night to price_per_night and stored the price directly in the Rooms table instead of having a separate price table. For this project, I thought that was a simpler solution because each room has its own price and a separate table would add unnecessary complexity.

I removed is_booked and is_cancelled from the Bookings table. Since each row already represents a booking, I did not think these extra columns were necessary. A room's availability can instead be determined by checking its bookings and dates. When a booking is cancelled, the booking can be deleted.

I also used UNIQUE constraints for customer email addresses and room numbers within the same hotel. Together with foreign keys, these help protect the data from duplicates and invalid relationships. One thing that took me a while to understand was foreign keys. At first, I found them confusing, but working with my own database helped me understand why they are important.

## Security, Results and Limitations

The result is a relational database connected to a C# console application. I used SQL JOIN queries to retrieve information from several tables when searching for hotels and rooms. I chose to let SQL handle much of the searching because the database can filter the information before it is returned to the application. The results can also be sorted by price or rating.

The application allows users to search using location, dates, number of people and different amenities. Users can also make bookings, view existing bookings, change the number of occupants and cancel bookings.

I tested the database connection and different search options, including location, dates, number of people and amenities such as a pool or restaurant. I also tested the booking functions, changing the number of occupants and cancelling a booking. I checked that the application rejected a number of occupants that was higher than the room's capacity and that a cancelled booking disappeared from the customer's booking list. I also restarted the application to check that bookings were still saved. Finally, I used dotnet build to make sure the project built without errors.

For security, I used parameterized SQL queries. This helps reduce the risk of SQL injection because user input is handled separately from the SQL command. The database constraints also help protect the information and keep relationships between tables correct.

There are still limitations. The application is a console application without a graphical interface or user accounts with different access permissions. It is mainly designed for one person to use at a time, and the booking dates are limited to December 2026. These are things that could be improved in a more complete booking system.

## Improvements

Booking dates are an important part of the project because a room should not be available when it is already booked. If I continued working on the project, I would spend more time testing different overlapping booking situations, including cases where one booking period is completely inside another. I would also test that a new customer can check in on the previous customer's checkout date.

I would also like to improve the application with a graphical interface, login functionality and different user permissions. Another improvement would be clearer error messages so users understand what went wrong and what they need to do.

## Use of AI and Other Support

I used ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot as support during the project. Since databases were completely new to me, I used them when I got stuck or needed help understanding parts of the booking logic. From my previous course, I had already learned how important it is to double-check AI suggestions instead of just trusting them. I kept that in mind during this project and tried not to simply copy suggestions. I wanted to understand what the code did and why a solution worked.

I checked the suggestions by reading and understanding the code, running SQL queries, testing the application and building the project myself. This helped me make sure that the suggestions actually worked in my own solution.

## Conclusion

I have learned a lot during these three weeks. I went from having very little knowledge of databases to creating tables, understanding relationships, writing SQL queries and connecting a database to a C# application.

The biggest challenge was understanding how all the different parts worked together. Over time, things became clearer, especially when I could try them in my own project.

I still have a lot to learn, but this project has given me a much better understanding of databases and a good foundation for future courses and projects.